gametitle=Champions: Return to Arms (SLUS-20973) v2.00

// fp-proposed.pnach - one section proposed for the fix set (patches/SLUS-20973_4028A55F.pnach), in its final form.
// It is NOT in that file and not in tools/play-net/sections-suggested.txt until it is adopted at a release;
// docs/issues/native-first-person-controls.md part 3 has the proof.  Every player of a game needs the same set of
// sections.  Written by local/recomp-fp-controls/work/fpasm.py (the words below are its output; each of its
// encoders is checked against a word of the game); tools/recomp/app/fp_section_test.py runs the section in the
// function-level interpreter.

[Net test: strafe mode - a player can run one way and face another]
author=first-person controls work, 2026-10-13; executed in the function-level interpreter (tools/recomp/app/fp_section_test.py); confirmed on two, three and four native copies in one lockstep game and on a native copy with an emulator-based copy (docs/issues/native-first-person-controls.md part 3); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=The game runs a character along its facing and turns the facing to the stick: nobody can run sideways or backwards while facing ahead, which a view from the character's eyes needs. This gives each player a mode byte (player object +0x47E, a byte only the unreachable jump state used). While the byte is 0 - for everyone who never asks for the mode - every tick is exactly as without this section. While it is not 0, in the ordinary moving state the character turns (40 % of the angle a tick, the game's own rate) to where its player's camera looks - the heading a pushed-up stick asks for, 0x3E3D minus the yaw word that already travels with every controller row - and runs along the stick's own direction instead of along its facing, at the same speed. The mode is set by game message 4, sub-type 2 {2, slot, mode}; message 4 is the game's own "null" channel, carried and executed in lockstep with an empty handler (its one sender in the game, sub-type 1 when a shop closes, is still ignored). Every machine sets the byte in the same tick. Player_ctor now clears the byte, so every new character (level start, respawn) starts with the mode off on every machine. Parts: the hook in Player_move (0x1B4210); its code in the unreferenced function 0x2128C8; a sender in the unreferenced function 0x215C98 (called with the mode in a0; only a build that offers the mode calls it); the handler in the unreferenced function 0x215D28, reached by a jump from the empty handler 0x1E01B0; and the two byte stores of the constructor. Every player must have this section or none.
// Player_move 0x1b4210; was: 24020005 12A2007C
// j 0x2128c8
